WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.127 --> 00:00:04.295
male narrator: This
is what the future of life

00:00:04.295 --> 00:00:07.382
off the Earth looks like.

00:00:07.382 --> 00:00:10.719
The moon is a
fully-developed commercial hub

00:00:10.719 --> 00:00:14.431
and way station for traveling
deeper into space.

00:00:14.431 --> 00:00:16.307
Anyone who wants a truly

00:00:16.307 --> 00:00:19.227
out-of-this-world
vacation destination

00:00:19.227 --> 00:00:23.481
can now look to the stars
and back down at Earth.

00:00:23.481 --> 00:00:25.984
The first permanent settlers
of Mars

00:00:25.984 --> 00:00:28.278
arrive
to self-building habitats

00:00:28.278 --> 00:00:31.823
that produce air, water,
and food.

00:00:31.823 --> 00:00:35.285
With a new generation destined
to be born on alien planets,

00:00:35.285 --> 00:00:38.997
this is the next step
in our evolution as a species.

00:00:38.997 --> 00:00:41.833
[soft dramatic music]

00:00:41.833 --> 00:00:43.418
- Well, this is
the coolest thing I've done

00:00:43.418 --> 00:00:45.045
in a long, long time.

00:00:45.045 --> 00:00:49.466
Today, scientists are blazing
a trail to this very future.

00:00:49.466 --> 00:00:51.301
- This is just
a remarkable structure

00:00:51.301 --> 00:00:53.053
from an engineering
perspective.

00:00:53.053 --> 00:00:54.345
- I wanna know

00:00:54.345 --> 00:00:55.930
what breakthroughs
are being made...

00:00:55.930 --> 00:00:58.183
- Water could be used
as rocket fuel,

00:00:58.183 --> 00:01:01.019
so we could use the moon
as a refueling station.

00:01:01.019 --> 00:01:03.688
- That is a big, hairy,
audacious goal.

00:01:03.688 --> 00:01:06.524
- That will forge
a future to...

00:01:06.524 --> 00:01:07.776
Here we go.

00:01:07.776 --> 00:01:09.986
Life off Earth.

00:01:09.986 --> 00:01:14.991
[dynamic dramatic music]

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:45.522
My name is Justin Shaifer

00:01:45.522 --> 00:01:47.899
and I'm
an environmental scientist.

00:01:47.899 --> 00:01:50.944
Since I was a kid growing up
on the South Side of Chicago,

00:01:50.944 --> 00:01:54.405
I've always dreamed
of exploring space.

00:01:54.405 --> 00:01:56.616
When it comes to the very
expensive prospect

00:01:56.616 --> 00:01:58.868
of sending humans
to live in space,

00:01:58.868 --> 00:02:01.788
many people ask,
"Why should we go?"

00:02:01.788 --> 00:02:04.707
But as someone that's
concerned about climate change

00:02:04.707 --> 00:02:06.751
and the health
of planet Earth,

00:02:06.751 --> 00:02:09.295
should we be thinking
about moving to another home?

00:02:09.295 --> 00:02:10.922
If so,

00:02:10.922 --> 00:02:13.925
how will we actually build
an outpost on an alien planet

00:02:13.925 --> 00:02:17.762
and then survive the lethal
environment of outer space?

00:02:17.762 --> 00:02:21.808
I'm in Columbus, Ohio, to meet
someone who knows first-hand,

00:02:21.808 --> 00:02:26.396
former NASA astronaut,
Dr. Kathryn Sullivan.

00:02:26.396 --> 00:02:30.650
Kathy was a truly
inspirational figure to me.

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:32.819
I was an intern at NOAA,

00:02:32.819 --> 00:02:35.363
the National Oceanic and
Atmospheric Administration,

00:02:35.363 --> 00:02:36.823
the first time we met.

00:02:36.823 --> 00:02:38.575
It was then that I saw

00:02:38.575 --> 00:02:41.911
the possibility
of being an astronaut

00:02:41.911 --> 00:02:43.913
as something
that was very real.

00:02:43.913 --> 00:02:49.335
Incredibly, this prospect
seems possible in my lifetime.

00:02:49.335 --> 00:02:52.630
To find out just how and when
humanity will accomplish this,

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:56.009
I'm catching up with Kathryn,
my old mentor.

00:02:58.011 --> 00:03:00.346
I just remember listening
to you speak.

00:03:00.346 --> 00:03:01.514
- Oh, cool.

00:03:01.514 --> 00:03:02.891
- It made me want
to be an astronaut.

00:03:02.891 --> 00:03:04.559
It made me think that it
was actually a feasible thing.

00:03:04.559 --> 00:03:06.853
- Well, thanks.
That makes me feel really good.

00:03:06.853 --> 00:03:09.189
Welcome to our planetarium.

00:03:09.189 --> 00:03:11.149
- Whoa.

00:03:11.149 --> 00:03:13.026
- We can project things
on this dome

00:03:13.026 --> 00:03:15.445
that will take you anywhere
in the universe.

00:03:15.445 --> 00:03:18.990
- Kathryn is a geologist,
deep sea explorer,

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:23.786
and was the first American
woman to walk in space.

00:03:23.786 --> 00:03:27.373
She knows a thing or two
about life off Earth.

00:03:27.373 --> 00:03:29.417
You spent a lot of time
in space, right?

00:03:29.417 --> 00:03:32.253
- My total is, like, 23 days.

00:03:32.253 --> 00:03:34.088
- Kathryn flew
multiple shuttle missions

00:03:34.088 --> 00:03:36.716
during her 15 years at NASA.

00:03:36.716 --> 00:03:38.509
- Once you're in orbit,

00:03:38.509 --> 00:03:40.220
you have effectively
no weight.

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:43.473
To be able to sort of drift
in the air like particle dust

00:03:43.473 --> 00:03:45.058
is pretty magical.

00:03:45.058 --> 00:03:47.769
- But space is dangerous,

00:03:47.769 --> 00:03:51.397
a vacuum devoid of atmosphere,
water, and oxygen,

00:03:51.397 --> 00:03:53.733
everything we need to survive.

00:03:53.733 --> 00:03:56.236
Do you think
the human body as is

00:03:56.236 --> 00:03:59.948
is designed to tolerate space
for years, decades?

00:03:59.948 --> 00:04:01.824
- There are definitely
some unknowns

00:04:01.824 --> 00:04:03.284
and some ifs about that.

00:04:03.284 --> 00:04:05.662
If our bodies are not sensing
a gravity field,

00:04:05.662 --> 00:04:07.163
the calcium in our bones,

00:04:07.163 --> 00:04:09.290
which gives our bones
their strength,

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:11.251
that starts to leech out.

00:04:11.251 --> 00:04:14.420
- Not only that--
absent protective gear,

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:17.924
long-term exposure
to radiation damages DNA

00:04:17.924 --> 00:04:19.509
and can lead to cancer.

00:04:19.509 --> 00:04:21.469
- You can certainly
lose your life in space

00:04:21.469 --> 00:04:22.637
and you've gotta be
aware of that

00:04:22.637 --> 00:04:24.764
and clear-minded
about the risks of it.

00:04:24.764 --> 00:04:27.725
- Much of what we know
about these dangers

00:04:27.725 --> 00:04:29.936
comes from studying
the long-term inhabitants

00:04:29.936 --> 00:04:32.772
of space stations orbiting
Earth.

00:04:32.772 --> 00:04:35.650
NASA astronaut Scott Kelly
spent nearly a year

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:39.279
on the International
Space Station, or ISS.

00:04:39.279 --> 00:04:40.947
As a result of this mission,

00:04:40.947 --> 00:04:43.283
Scott experienced
cognitive decline,

00:04:43.283 --> 00:04:48.371
damage to his eyes,
and even changes in his DNA.

00:04:48.371 --> 00:04:50.456
Before we can attempt
to build permanent bases

00:04:50.456 --> 00:04:51.708
on the moon or Mars,

00:04:51.708 --> 00:04:53.418
we're going to have
to figure out

00:04:53.418 --> 00:04:56.796
how to protect our bodies and
minds from threats like these.

00:04:56.796 --> 00:04:59.632
- That's one small step
for a man,

00:04:59.632 --> 00:05:03.094
one giant leap for mankind.

00:05:03.094 --> 00:05:05.430
- But where there's
a will, there's a way.

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:07.348
[soft dramatic music]

00:05:07.348 --> 00:05:11.394
- Mars now is, I think,
like the moon was in the 1960s:

00:05:11.394 --> 00:05:13.730
A big, hairy, audacious goal.

00:05:13.730 --> 00:05:15.898
I definitely think
exploring Mars

00:05:15.898 --> 00:05:18.359
and figuring out what would
it take to get there--

00:05:18.359 --> 00:05:20.528
I'd love to see us do that.

00:05:20.528 --> 00:05:24.365
I think it's really wired into
human nature to be exploring.

00:05:24.365 --> 00:05:26.868
To not explore our place
in the cosmos,

00:05:26.868 --> 00:05:29.120
to not continue
to expand our understanding

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:32.665
at the largest scale
of where we live

00:05:32.665 --> 00:05:34.667
and our place in the universe,

00:05:34.667 --> 00:05:36.419
I think is just short-sighted.

00:05:36.419 --> 00:05:41.215
- Three, two, one, zero.

00:05:41.215 --> 00:05:43.301
Liftoff.
We have a liftoff.

00:05:43.301 --> 00:05:44.761
- With so many unknowns

00:05:44.761 --> 00:05:47.722
surrounding the welfare
of humans in deep space,

00:05:47.722 --> 00:05:50.224
NASA is first setting
its sights on a return trip

00:05:50.224 --> 00:05:52.393
to the moon.

00:05:52.393 --> 00:05:55.980
The Artemis mission,
scheduled to launch by 2024,

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:57.398
is the first step towards

00:05:57.398 --> 00:06:00.526
establishing a permanent
human presence at the moon

00:06:00.526 --> 00:06:03.154
with the ultimate goal
of using it as a springboard

00:06:03.154 --> 00:06:05.490
to the red planet.

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:07.325
A spacecraft orbiting the moon

00:06:07.325 --> 00:06:10.703
will serve as a hub for
research on the lunar surface.

00:06:10.703 --> 00:06:13.039
The goal is to learn
how to live and operate

00:06:13.039 --> 00:06:14.582
on another celestial body
while

00:06:14.582 --> 00:06:18.711
proving technologies necessary
for a mission to Mars.

00:06:18.711 --> 00:06:20.213
But at present,

00:06:20.213 --> 00:06:22.924
lifting people and materials
above Earth's atmosphere

00:06:22.924 --> 00:06:25.301
is extremely expensive.

00:06:25.301 --> 00:06:28.262
To build a moon base, we'll
need to find cheaper methods

00:06:28.262 --> 00:06:31.766
and reusable equipment.

00:06:31.766 --> 00:06:34.852
I'm in Colorado to visit
Sierra Nevada Corporation

00:06:34.852 --> 00:06:39.023
who is working with NASA
on this heavy duty problem.

00:06:39.023 --> 00:06:41.109
- Welcome
to Sierra Nevada Corporation,

00:06:41.109 --> 00:06:42.360
home of Dream Chaser.

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:44.404
- Wow.
It's beautiful.

00:06:44.404 --> 00:06:47.323
John Curry is the director
of the Dream Chaser project.

00:06:47.323 --> 00:06:49.075
- Dream Chaser
is a space plane,

00:06:49.075 --> 00:06:50.785
so it's just like riding
on an airplane

00:06:50.785 --> 00:06:52.703
landing horizontally
on a runway.

00:06:52.703 --> 00:06:54.997
As you know, the space
shuttle flew 135 missions

00:06:54.997 --> 00:06:57.542
and it worked very well.

00:06:57.542 --> 00:06:59.085
What makes Dream Chaser
different

00:06:59.085 --> 00:07:00.795
is it is so much cheaper.

00:07:00.795 --> 00:07:03.423
The space shuttle was just
too expensive to maintain.

00:07:03.423 --> 00:07:04.715
We're lighter weight,

00:07:04.715 --> 00:07:06.592
we're stronger
than the shuttle.

00:07:06.592 --> 00:07:08.428
- The Dream Chaser's
clever design

00:07:08.428 --> 00:07:11.556
and carbon fiber body
allows it to hold enough cargo

00:07:11.556 --> 00:07:16.227
to supply astronauts
on the ISS for half a year.

00:07:16.227 --> 00:07:18.229
With innovations like these,

00:07:18.229 --> 00:07:21.232
it can be flown 15 times
before maintenance,

00:07:21.232 --> 00:07:23.234
keeping costs down.

00:07:23.234 --> 00:07:27.155
The space shuttle required
maintenance after each launch.

00:07:27.155 --> 00:07:30.533
With the retirement
of the space shuttle in 2011,

00:07:30.533 --> 00:07:32.910
the Dream Chaser
is now the only spacecraft

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:35.413
capable of delivering people,
supplies,

00:07:35.413 --> 00:07:38.207
and science experiments
to the ISS

00:07:38.207 --> 00:07:40.334
and then navigating back
to Earth

00:07:40.334 --> 00:07:42.628
for a gentle runway landing.

00:07:42.628 --> 00:07:45.339
It can also perform the whole
mission autonomously--

00:07:45.339 --> 00:07:46.549
without a pilot.

00:07:46.549 --> 00:07:49.260
- I believe this vehicle
is gonna be critical

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:53.764
to transporting humans to and
from space on a routine basis.

00:07:53.764 --> 00:07:55.266
- Sierra Nevada plans
to conduct

00:07:55.266 --> 00:07:59.145
six ISS resupply missions
by 2024.

00:07:59.145 --> 00:08:00.646
And unlike the shuttle,

00:08:00.646 --> 00:08:03.608
which launched aside
two boosters and a fuel tank,

00:08:03.608 --> 00:08:05.151
the Dream Chaser's designed

00:08:05.151 --> 00:08:09.155
to be launched into orbit
wings folded atop a rocket.

00:08:09.155 --> 00:08:10.573
[rocket whooshing]

00:08:10.573 --> 00:08:11.782
So what's the top speed

00:08:11.782 --> 00:08:12.867
something like this
would reach?

00:08:12.950 --> 00:08:16.078
- Orbital velocity
is 17,500 miles an hour.

00:08:16.078 --> 00:08:17.622
- Dream Chaser
will circle the Earth

00:08:17.622 --> 00:08:19.832
16 times a day at this speed,

00:08:19.832 --> 00:08:22.627
but when it returns
and enters the atmosphere,

00:08:22.627 --> 00:08:25.421
it practically has
to grind to a halt.

00:08:25.421 --> 00:08:26.839
- What's cool about this

00:08:26.839 --> 00:08:28.257
and very innovative
about this design

00:08:28.257 --> 00:08:29.717
is this is called
a lifting body,

00:08:29.717 --> 00:08:31.761
meaning the lift that you get
from his vehicle

00:08:31.761 --> 00:08:34.305
is actually the body itself
rather than the wings.

00:08:34.305 --> 00:08:36.224
- Unlike most planes,

00:08:36.224 --> 00:08:39.435
Dream Chaser's actual body,
not its wings,

00:08:39.435 --> 00:08:41.312
takes the full force
of the air pressure.

00:08:41.312 --> 00:08:44.815
The underside of the
Dream Chaser is wide and flat

00:08:44.815 --> 00:08:48.194
and pushes against the
atmosphere to slow reentry.

00:08:48.194 --> 00:08:50.696
- Probably the biggest risk
for Dream Chaser is the entry

00:08:50.696 --> 00:08:52.698
'cause you see
3,000 degrees Fahrenheit

00:08:52.698 --> 00:08:54.116
coming back
through the atmosphere

00:08:54.116 --> 00:08:55.868
to get to the ground,
so you gotta make sure

00:08:55.868 --> 00:08:59.914
that this vehicle can survive
3,000 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:09:00.039 --> 00:09:01.541
- The Dream Chaser
can land anywhere

00:09:01.541 --> 00:09:04.335
there is a 10,000-foot
long runway,

00:09:04.335 --> 00:09:05.545
which means it can return

00:09:05.545 --> 00:09:07.922
to any major airport
in the world.

00:09:07.922 --> 00:09:11.801
[tires squeal]

00:09:11.801 --> 00:09:13.636
For the Artemis moon mission,

00:09:13.636 --> 00:09:16.013
NASA is looking
at a radical technology

00:09:16.013 --> 00:09:19.892
to house the astronauts:
Inflatable habitats.

00:09:19.892 --> 00:09:23.104
These habitats will connect
to a core power module

00:09:23.104 --> 00:09:25.356
dubbed "Gateway."

00:09:25.356 --> 00:09:26.983
Sierra Nevada designed
a system

00:09:26.983 --> 00:09:30.861
called the Large Inflatable
Fabric Environment, or LIFE,

00:09:30.861 --> 00:09:32.947
that can dock
to the Gateway module.

00:09:32.947 --> 00:09:34.323
- We have a module

00:09:34.323 --> 00:09:36.075
you could actually
turn into a space station,

00:09:36.075 --> 00:09:37.994
and that would be what you
would want to take to the moon.

00:09:37.994 --> 00:09:40.830
- The habitat can
be folded in a compact manner

00:09:40.830 --> 00:09:44.208
for launch and then
later expanded in space.

00:09:44.208 --> 00:09:47.044
Once connected
to NASA's Gateway module,

00:09:47.044 --> 00:09:49.922
inflatable habitats will serve
as a base for researchers

00:09:49.922 --> 00:09:52.258
traveling
to and from the moon.

00:09:52.258 --> 00:09:53.801
- The key elements
to get us to the moon

00:09:53.801 --> 00:09:56.304
is we have to put
the infrastructure in place

00:09:56.304 --> 00:09:59.265
to make sure that people
can actually stay there.

00:09:59.265 --> 00:10:00.683
Once you've got that,

00:10:00.683 --> 00:10:02.852
then you have
a permanent presence in space.

00:10:02.852 --> 00:10:05.354
- Technologies like
these are forging the path

00:10:05.354 --> 00:10:09.191
for humans to build
a permanent home off Earth.

00:10:11.902 --> 00:10:15.406
narrator: In the future,
inflatable lunar habitats

00:10:15.406 --> 00:10:17.700
prove safe
and reliable gateways

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:19.619
to the moon's surface.

00:10:19.619 --> 00:10:21.078
On a weekly basis,

00:10:21.078 --> 00:10:23.497
researchers shuttle
between the moon and Earth

00:10:23.497 --> 00:10:27.251
on advanced space planes
with enhanced rocket boosts.

00:10:27.251 --> 00:10:31.631
Travel time is reduced
over 400,000-kilometer journey

00:10:31.631 --> 00:10:35.259
from four days to just one.

00:10:35.259 --> 00:10:38.012
With the completion
of a permanent research base

00:10:38.012 --> 00:10:39.597
on the surface of the moon,

00:10:39.597 --> 00:10:42.099
space agencies approve
both the base

00:10:42.099 --> 00:10:44.977
and the orbiting station
for commercial uses.

00:10:44.977 --> 00:10:49.273
Space tourists can even enjoy
a one-week stay.

00:10:51.108 --> 00:10:53.527
- With inflatable
habitat technology,

00:10:53.527 --> 00:10:55.196
a permanent presence
on the moon

00:10:55.196 --> 00:10:58.949
might become a reality sooner
than we think,

00:10:58.949 --> 00:11:02.119
but with virtually
no atmosphere,

00:11:02.119 --> 00:11:04.413
exploration
of the lunar surface

00:11:04.413 --> 00:11:06.248
will require new technologies

00:11:06.248 --> 00:11:11.045
to protect explorers from
the sun's lethal radiation.

00:11:11.045 --> 00:11:13.881
The last spacesuits designed
to function on the moon

00:11:13.881 --> 00:11:16.384
were custom-fabricated
for the Apollo astronauts

00:11:16.384 --> 00:11:18.594
more than 50 years ago.

00:11:18.594 --> 00:11:21.806
These suits were both
expensive and cumbersome.

00:11:23.349 --> 00:11:26.102
I'm in Brooklyn, New York,
to meet Ted Southern,

00:11:26.102 --> 00:11:29.063
the co-founder
of Final Frontier design.

00:11:29.063 --> 00:11:32.233
His company is developing the
next generation of spacesuits

00:11:32.233 --> 00:11:34.860
designed to overcome
these limitations.

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:37.405
Whoa.
This is pretty cool.

00:11:37.405 --> 00:11:39.532
- This is where
we make spacesuits.

00:11:39.532 --> 00:11:41.909
Generally during prototyping
of suits,

00:11:41.909 --> 00:11:45.037
we work with NASA and
the commercial space industry

00:11:45.037 --> 00:11:47.289
to make suits work better.

00:11:47.289 --> 00:11:51.127
This is an EVA suit.
Extravehicular Activity.

00:11:51.127 --> 00:11:52.878
So this is when
you leave the vehicle.

00:11:52.878 --> 00:11:56.132
It's like a space ship
for one person.

00:11:56.132 --> 00:11:57.591
- In the days of Apollo,

00:11:57.591 --> 00:12:01.053
spacesuits were only worn
for a single mission.

00:12:01.053 --> 00:12:03.723
Final Frontier
is designing reusable suits

00:12:03.723 --> 00:12:06.767
that are flexible,
lightweight, and durable.

00:12:06.767 --> 00:12:08.644
- Now, NASA's talking about
back to the moon,

00:12:08.644 --> 00:12:11.814
but for a lot longer period
of time,

00:12:11.814 --> 00:12:14.108
so they want suits
that are more mobile,

00:12:14.108 --> 00:12:17.069
that won't wear you out,
and that can last longer.

00:12:17.069 --> 00:12:20.531
There's no reason for a human
to go the moon or Mars.

00:12:20.531 --> 00:12:22.867
Unless they can get outside
and walk around.

00:12:24.410 --> 00:12:27.079
- The key to surviving
extreme environments like this

00:12:27.079 --> 00:12:29.832
lies in the pressurized
EVA suit.

00:12:29.832 --> 00:12:31.083
- It's like wearing
a basketball,

00:12:31.083 --> 00:12:32.877
which is where the challenge
of, like,

00:12:32.877 --> 00:12:34.837
how do you get a basketball
to fit a human being

00:12:34.837 --> 00:12:37.715
and how do you get it
to move like a human being?

00:12:37.715 --> 00:12:39.884
- Pressurization is essential

00:12:39.884 --> 00:12:42.011
to surviving
in the vacuum of space.

00:12:42.011 --> 00:12:44.221
Without it,
blood would fill with bubbles

00:12:44.221 --> 00:12:47.224
and the body would puff up
to twice its size,

00:12:47.224 --> 00:12:50.686
causing death
in less than two minutes.

00:12:50.686 --> 00:12:52.646
But pressuring the suit

00:12:52.646 --> 00:12:55.274
to protect the wearer
from the harshness of space

00:12:55.274 --> 00:12:57.860
creates a problem
with agility.

00:12:57.860 --> 00:12:59.403
So you lose a lot of dexterity?

00:12:59.403 --> 00:13:01.989
- Dexterity, tactility,
range of motion.

00:13:01.989 --> 00:13:03.824
All these things
are really important.

00:13:03.824 --> 00:13:06.994
We actually have a tool that
allows us to test the gloves

00:13:06.994 --> 00:13:08.412
without having
to put on a whole suit.

00:13:08.412 --> 00:13:09.705
- Oh, really?
Nice.

00:13:09.705 --> 00:13:11.832
- Want to check that out?
- Yeah.

00:13:11.832 --> 00:13:14.627
Final Frontier won a prize
from NASA

00:13:14.627 --> 00:13:17.588
for their superior
pressurized glove design.

00:13:17.588 --> 00:13:21.550
- This is a pressure chamber
and there's a pump underneath

00:13:21.550 --> 00:13:24.595
that will pull gas out
from the chamber.

00:13:24.595 --> 00:13:26.138
Stick your hands in
through the ports.

00:13:26.138 --> 00:13:27.681
- The chamber is designed

00:13:27.681 --> 00:13:30.476
to create the same extreme
vacuum of being in space.

00:13:30.476 --> 00:13:33.896
- You're at about four PSI
lower than ambient.

00:13:33.896 --> 00:13:36.816
You're approaching NASA's
EVA spacesuit pressure.

00:13:36.816 --> 00:13:38.150
- Hmm.

00:13:38.192 --> 00:13:40.444
Okay, so this is the problem
that astronauts have.

00:13:40.444 --> 00:13:41.570
- Exactly, yeah.

00:13:41.654 --> 00:13:42.947
- Almost feels like
I have a robot hand.

00:13:42.947 --> 00:13:43.948
Yeah, it's--

00:13:43.948 --> 00:13:44.990
- I'd really appreciate it

00:13:44.990 --> 00:13:45.991
if you solved that
Rubik's Cube, too.

00:13:45.991 --> 00:13:48.035
- [chuckling]

00:13:48.035 --> 00:13:49.745
Let's see.

00:13:49.745 --> 00:13:52.122
Do you think that there's
anything wrong

00:13:52.122 --> 00:13:54.750
how these things should
be designed for people?

00:13:54.750 --> 00:13:56.085
- Yes.
[chuckles]

00:13:56.085 --> 00:13:59.713
Um, spacesuits
are traditionally built

00:13:59.713 --> 00:14:01.257
like big bubbles.

00:14:01.257 --> 00:14:02.800
Theoretically,
there are garments

00:14:02.800 --> 00:14:04.635
that could tighten
around your body

00:14:04.635 --> 00:14:06.887
equivalent to the gas
that you're breathing,

00:14:06.887 --> 00:14:09.348
which would be
much tighter-fitting,

00:14:09.348 --> 00:14:11.100
potentially more mobility.

00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:14.562
That could revolutionize
how spacesuits work.

00:14:14.562 --> 00:14:16.522
We're gonna let air back
into the chamber.

00:14:16.522 --> 00:14:18.023
[air whooshes]
- Oh, wow.

00:14:18.023 --> 00:14:20.985
That definitely changed
very quickly.

00:14:20.985 --> 00:14:23.696
But that's not the only
problem they're solving.

00:14:23.696 --> 00:14:26.866
Moon dust is sharp
and very fine-grained.

00:14:26.866 --> 00:14:28.659
The particles get everywhere

00:14:28.659 --> 00:14:32.204
and can cause life-threatening
injury if inhaled.

00:14:32.204 --> 00:14:34.957
Final Frontiers EVA suit
has a unique feature

00:14:34.957 --> 00:14:36.458
to prevent this
from happening.

00:14:36.458 --> 00:14:38.711
The rear port
can dock directly

00:14:38.711 --> 00:14:41.005
with the landing craft
or rover.

00:14:41.005 --> 00:14:43.215
Is this, like, an entrance
on the back side?

00:14:43.215 --> 00:14:44.216
Is that how it's--
- That's right, yeah.

00:14:44.216 --> 00:14:45.509
You get in through the back.

00:14:45.509 --> 00:14:48.345
This'll allow an interface
with a suit port

00:14:48.345 --> 00:14:51.348
so you can be inside your rover
and jump in the space suit

00:14:51.348 --> 00:14:53.225
without bringing dust
into the rover.

00:14:53.225 --> 00:14:55.269
It's also easier
on the astronauts

00:14:55.269 --> 00:14:56.854
to get in and out this way.
- Mm-hmm.

00:14:56.854 --> 00:14:59.356
- NASA's current suits,
you get in through the waist

00:14:59.356 --> 00:15:00.858
and that means you have
to really--

00:15:00.858 --> 00:15:02.318
- Wiggle in there?
- Contort your shoulders.

00:15:02.318 --> 00:15:03.527
- It's like a dance move,
kinda?

00:15:03.527 --> 00:15:04.904
- [chuckling]
Yeah.

00:15:04.904 --> 00:15:06.363
And there are a lot of
astronaut injuries from suits,

00:15:06.363 --> 00:15:08.824
but one of the most major ones
is shoulder injuries.

00:15:08.824 --> 00:15:12.119
- From getting into--yes.
- From getting into the suit.

00:15:12.119 --> 00:15:14.038
- Ted's got a surprise for me.

00:15:14.038 --> 00:15:16.624
He's letting me try on
the pressure suit,

00:15:16.624 --> 00:15:17.875
which is something
that usually

00:15:17.875 --> 00:15:19.877
only astronauts get
to experience.

00:15:19.877 --> 00:15:22.212
- Right now,
we have air flowing in

00:15:22.212 --> 00:15:24.590
through the umbilicals here
on your chest.

00:15:24.590 --> 00:15:25.925
The only place
it'll come out of

00:15:25.925 --> 00:15:28.761
is right here
at your regulator.

00:15:28.761 --> 00:15:31.180
If you kind of
push your head back.

00:15:31.180 --> 00:15:34.350
- With my pressure balanced,
it's time to experience

00:15:34.350 --> 00:15:37.728
what walking on the moon
or Mars might be like.

00:15:37.728 --> 00:15:39.188
- Try walking around in there.

00:15:39.188 --> 00:15:41.941
[dubstep music]

00:15:41.941 --> 00:15:44.443
- I have no--I feel like
I have no joints right now.

00:15:44.443 --> 00:15:45.778
- Yeah?
[chuckles]

00:15:45.778 --> 00:15:48.822
- I'm like a tin man.
- [chuckles]

00:15:48.822 --> 00:15:52.242
- [chuckling]

00:15:52.242 --> 00:15:54.578
Well, this is the coolest thing
I've done in a long, long time.

00:15:54.578 --> 00:15:57.247
I don't know if I've ever done
anything cooler than this.

00:15:57.247 --> 00:16:00.960
With advances in new, flexible
protective suits like these...

00:16:00.960 --> 00:16:02.628
- Hey.
Welcome back.

00:16:02.628 --> 00:16:06.465
- The path to exploring
other planets seems clear.

00:16:06.465 --> 00:16:09.093
[soft dramatic music]

00:16:09.093 --> 00:16:10.302
narrator:
In the future,

00:16:10.302 --> 00:16:12.972
EVA suits
for space exploration

00:16:12.972 --> 00:16:16.642
are as flexible
and comfortable as wetsuits.

00:16:16.642 --> 00:16:19.228
Skin-tight
elastic undergarments

00:16:19.228 --> 00:16:22.564
apply stable mechanical
pressure against the skin,

00:16:22.564 --> 00:16:26.193
eliminating the need
for air pressurization.

00:16:26.193 --> 00:16:29.863
These suits also compensate
for lower gravitational pull,

00:16:29.863 --> 00:16:32.032
allowing scientists
to explore

00:16:32.032 --> 00:16:34.451
just as easily
as they can do on Earth.

00:16:40.416 --> 00:16:43.752
- Even with protection
from the harshness of space,

00:16:43.752 --> 00:16:46.672
astronauts won't survive
a week on an alien planet

00:16:46.672 --> 00:16:48.632
without one living essential:

00:16:48.632 --> 00:16:50.134
Water.

00:16:50.134 --> 00:16:53.137
But the cost of bringing
enough liquid water from Earth

00:16:53.137 --> 00:16:56.140
to support even a small base
on the moon or Mars

00:16:56.140 --> 00:16:57.766
is astronomical.

00:16:57.766 --> 00:17:01.562
This means if we're going to
establish outposts in space,

00:17:01.562 --> 00:17:02.938
scientists must find

00:17:02.938 --> 00:17:05.899
a reliable source
of water elsewhere.

00:17:05.899 --> 00:17:10.279
I'm near LA to meet Dr. Dean
Bergman at Honeybee Robotics.

00:17:10.279 --> 00:17:13.449
Honeybee Robotics is
developing cutting-edge tools

00:17:13.449 --> 00:17:17.828
to help NASA find and extract
water from the moon or Mars.

00:17:17.828 --> 00:17:20.748
Oh, nice.

00:17:20.748 --> 00:17:22.708
- Why don't we go look
at some cool things?

00:17:22.708 --> 00:17:24.543
- All right.

00:17:24.543 --> 00:17:27.254
- We are developing
these technologies

00:17:27.254 --> 00:17:30.966
to be able to create
a sustainable civilization

00:17:30.966 --> 00:17:32.843
off of our planet.

00:17:32.843 --> 00:17:35.721
Water is the most
precious resource in space

00:17:35.721 --> 00:17:38.265
just because we can use it
for so much.

00:17:38.265 --> 00:17:40.017
And the more we can collect,

00:17:40.017 --> 00:17:44.146
the more ability we'd have
to really live off-world

00:17:44.146 --> 00:17:47.733
and on other planets.

00:17:47.733 --> 00:17:51.445
- In 2009, NASA confirmed the
presence of water on the moon

00:17:51.445 --> 00:17:53.363
in the form of ice.

00:17:53.363 --> 00:17:56.033
Some estimate
more than 100 million tons

00:17:56.033 --> 00:17:57.743
might be found
under the surface

00:17:57.743 --> 00:17:59.578
at the moon's south pole.

00:17:59.578 --> 00:18:02.498
Not only will
future spacefaring pioneers

00:18:02.498 --> 00:18:05.084
need this water
to drink and grow food,

00:18:05.084 --> 00:18:06.460
this essential liquid

00:18:06.460 --> 00:18:09.755
has another potential
important use in space.

00:18:09.755 --> 00:18:12.883
By splitting water
into hydrogen and oxygen,

00:18:12.883 --> 00:18:14.301
both of these molecules

00:18:14.301 --> 00:18:17.763
could be used
as propellant for spacecraft.

00:18:17.763 --> 00:18:20.015
- Water can also be used
as rocket fuel,

00:18:20.015 --> 00:18:22.392
so we could use the moon
as a refueling station

00:18:22.392 --> 00:18:24.770
on the way to Mars,
potentially.

00:18:24.770 --> 00:18:28.398
- Honeybee's drills were sent
to Mars on multiple missions

00:18:28.398 --> 00:18:31.360
to determine
what was below the surface.

00:18:31.360 --> 00:18:34.696
In 2002,
the Mars Odyssey orbiter

00:18:34.696 --> 00:18:38.033
detected large amounts of
water ice beneath the surface.

00:18:39.576 --> 00:18:41.161
So in 2007,

00:18:41.161 --> 00:18:44.748
NASA launched the Phoenix
Mars Lander to search for it.

00:18:44.748 --> 00:18:47.501
- We were part
of the Phoenix mission.

00:18:47.501 --> 00:18:51.338
The idea was to uncover
the ice of the poles of Mars.

00:18:51.338 --> 00:18:54.633
So we would scratch a line
on the surface

00:18:54.633 --> 00:18:56.135
and uncover the ice.
- Wow.

00:18:56.135 --> 00:18:58.554
Well, this is a powerful thing
to be in the presence of.

00:18:58.554 --> 00:19:00.013
- [chuckles] Yeah.

00:19:00.013 --> 00:19:01.473
- And the Phoenix mission

00:19:01.473 --> 00:19:03.559
discovered what
they were looking for:

00:19:03.559 --> 00:19:08.147
The first direct evidence
of frozen water on Mars.

00:19:08.147 --> 00:19:13.152
In 2018, the European Space
Agency's Mars Express orbiter

00:19:13.152 --> 00:19:15.028
discovered liquid water
for the first time

00:19:15.028 --> 00:19:17.281
beneath the south pole region,

00:19:17.281 --> 00:19:18.949
so Honeybee
is developing a method

00:19:18.949 --> 00:19:22.536
to extract the liquid water
with this: The TRIDENT drill

00:19:22.536 --> 00:19:27.124
with Planetary Volatiles
Extractor, or PVEx,

00:19:27.124 --> 00:19:30.127
and Vince Vendiola
is showing me how it works.

00:19:30.127 --> 00:19:32.379
- So this is Honeybee's
large vacuum chamber.

00:19:32.379 --> 00:19:35.257
It is a one meter by one meter
by three meter tall

00:19:35.257 --> 00:19:36.925
thermal vacuum chamber.

00:19:36.925 --> 00:19:38.343
PVEx is a coring drill

00:19:38.343 --> 00:19:40.596
with integrated heaters
and electronics

00:19:40.596 --> 00:19:43.765
to be able to capture water
from lunar ice.

00:19:43.765 --> 00:19:45.726
- Very cool stuff.

00:19:45.726 --> 00:19:48.103
The large vacuum chamber
is used to recreate

00:19:48.103 --> 00:19:50.647
the same low-pressure
atmospheric conditions

00:19:50.647 --> 00:19:52.691
found on Mars.

00:19:52.691 --> 00:19:56.862
- Well, let's do a drill test.
Close the chamber here.

00:19:56.862 --> 00:19:58.155
- Here we go.

00:19:58.155 --> 00:20:00.824
- I'm gonna lower the foot pad
onto the surface.

00:20:03.702 --> 00:20:05.245
- This hollow core drill

00:20:05.245 --> 00:20:07.789
can dig into the lunar
or Marian underground

00:20:07.789 --> 00:20:10.083
and collect water ice
and icy soil

00:20:10.083 --> 00:20:11.919
meters beneath the surface.

00:20:11.919 --> 00:20:13.962
[metallic tapping]
What's that noise?

00:20:13.962 --> 00:20:17.132
- That percussion sound
is our cam spring mechanism

00:20:17.132 --> 00:20:19.218
which gives us an additional
weight on the bit.

00:20:19.218 --> 00:20:21.136
- It's kind of like
a space jackhammer.

00:20:21.136 --> 00:20:22.971
- It's like a space jackhammer.

00:20:22.971 --> 00:20:24.223
And our software is designed

00:20:24.223 --> 00:20:26.558
so where we only percuss
when the system detects

00:20:26.558 --> 00:20:28.101
that you're drilling
into something hard

00:20:28.101 --> 00:20:29.645
so that we save a lot
of energy.

00:20:29.645 --> 00:20:31.855
- Hmm. So it's taking
a core sample right now?

00:20:31.855 --> 00:20:33.440
- Right.

00:20:33.440 --> 00:20:35.734
So we capture our sample cores
and then we activate heaters.

00:20:35.734 --> 00:20:38.487
- It's a process
to extract liquid water

00:20:38.487 --> 00:20:40.364
on the moon or Mars.

00:20:40.364 --> 00:20:43.617
First, the heaters
must vaporize the ice.

00:20:43.617 --> 00:20:46.954
Then the vapor would be
collected within a cold trap

00:20:46.954 --> 00:20:49.998
and converted into solid ice
for storage.

00:20:49.998 --> 00:20:52.251
Without this system,
the liquid water

00:20:52.251 --> 00:20:55.879
would instantly evaporate
and be lost.

00:20:55.879 --> 00:20:57.839
All right, is there water
in there now, or?

00:20:57.839 --> 00:20:59.299
- Let's see what we got.
- Okay.

00:20:59.299 --> 00:21:01.301
- Open up the chamber.

00:21:01.301 --> 00:21:04.388
[soft suspenseful music]

00:21:04.388 --> 00:21:07.391
- The epic reveal.

00:21:10.686 --> 00:21:14.231
- Hey, there we go.
- Oh, nice, nice.

00:21:14.231 --> 00:21:16.733
This is the future right here.

00:21:16.733 --> 00:21:19.194
The red planet has plenty
of ice,

00:21:19.194 --> 00:21:22.322
some buried
just inches below the surface.

00:21:22.322 --> 00:21:24.866
Using this technology
to extract it

00:21:24.866 --> 00:21:29.788
will make a future life
off Earth possible.

00:21:29.788 --> 00:21:31.707
narrator:
In the future,

00:21:31.707 --> 00:21:34.209
advanced robotic
drilling technology

00:21:34.209 --> 00:21:37.462
sets off a gold rush in space.

00:21:37.462 --> 00:21:40.716
Entrepreneurs race
to prospect asteroids

00:21:40.716 --> 00:21:43.468
and comets
across the solar system.

00:21:43.468 --> 00:21:46.972
Autonomous probes mine
these celestial bodies

00:21:46.972 --> 00:21:49.766
for precious metals
and water ice.

00:21:49.766 --> 00:21:52.519
Once the ice is converted
into rocket fuel,

00:21:52.519 --> 00:21:55.022
it is more valuable than gold.

00:21:55.022 --> 00:21:57.232
Trading posts
and way stations emerge

00:21:57.232 --> 00:22:01.028
across the solar system
to process this precious cargo

00:22:01.028 --> 00:22:04.364
and further the exploration
of space.

00:22:04.364 --> 00:22:07.367
[peaceful music]

00:22:07.367 --> 00:22:10.954
- With a large source of
water ice identified on Mars,

00:22:10.954 --> 00:22:12.914
building
a long-term base there

00:22:12.914 --> 00:22:15.250
sounds more and more viable.

00:22:17.169 --> 00:22:19.713
But what other problems
will scientists need to solve

00:22:19.713 --> 00:22:22.299
before people can live
in a hostile environment

00:22:22.299 --> 00:22:25.844
on missions that could
stretch for years?

00:22:28.513 --> 00:22:31.850
Yeah, we are definitely
on another planet right now.

00:22:31.850 --> 00:22:35.896
I'm in a desolate part
of Wayne County, Utah.

00:22:35.896 --> 00:22:39.274
Here it certainly looks
like Mars.

00:22:39.274 --> 00:22:41.693
I'm heading to the
Mars Desert Research Center

00:22:41.693 --> 00:22:46.156
to meet commander
Marc Levesque and his team.

00:22:47.657 --> 00:22:48.909
[door latch squeaks]

00:22:48.909 --> 00:22:50.619
Hi, how's it going?
- Welcome.

00:22:50.619 --> 00:22:53.580
Let's head on upstairs.
- All right.

00:22:55.540 --> 00:22:57.250
Hey, folks.
- Nice to meet you.

00:22:57.250 --> 00:22:58.627
- Rich?
- Rich.

00:22:58.627 --> 00:23:02.506
- And we have, uh, a nice,
international crew here.

00:23:02.506 --> 00:23:05.425
- This habitat serves
as an Earth analogue

00:23:05.425 --> 00:23:08.637
of an early manned
research base on Mars.

00:23:08.637 --> 00:23:10.555
It contains
the essential components

00:23:10.555 --> 00:23:12.933
a future team would need
for a long-term mission

00:23:12.933 --> 00:23:16.728
including living quarters,
greenhouse, science labs,

00:23:16.728 --> 00:23:18.563
and observatories.

00:23:18.563 --> 00:23:21.316
- There's a variety
of research that goes on here.

00:23:21.316 --> 00:23:26.321
Psychological, geological,
astronomical.

00:23:26.321 --> 00:23:28.407
- The water-parched extremes

00:23:28.407 --> 00:23:32.285
of the Mars Desert
Research Station, or MDRS,

00:23:32.285 --> 00:23:35.789
make it perfect for simulating
a mission on Mars.

00:23:35.789 --> 00:23:39.668
- This is one of the closest
analogues to Mars on Earth

00:23:39.668 --> 00:23:42.963
given the landscape,
given the isolation,

00:23:42.963 --> 00:23:45.966
given
the sporadic communications.

00:23:45.966 --> 00:23:49.302
- At the MDRS,
every effort is made

00:23:49.302 --> 00:23:52.222
to simulate the challenges
of conducting research

00:23:52.222 --> 00:23:54.224
in a hostile,
alien environment.

00:23:54.224 --> 00:23:58.770
- Any outside activity
such as exploring the area

00:23:58.770 --> 00:24:00.647
or conducting projects
out there,

00:24:00.647 --> 00:24:03.608
we have to be
in space suits

00:24:03.608 --> 00:24:06.445
and we have to go through
a depressurization

00:24:06.445 --> 00:24:08.697
for five minutes
in the airlock.

00:24:08.697 --> 00:24:10.157
- Science time.

00:24:10.157 --> 00:24:11.700
[soft dramatic music]

00:24:11.700 --> 00:24:13.034
- Okay, Justin.

00:24:13.034 --> 00:24:14.453
We're gonna take
two rovers with us.

00:24:14.453 --> 00:24:16.371
And these two guys are gonna go
in the second rover.

00:24:16.371 --> 00:24:17.622
- Roger that.

00:24:17.622 --> 00:24:19.416
- Hello, Hab.
This is Ares 1.

00:24:19.416 --> 00:24:21.501
That's us entering
the airlock now, over.

00:24:23.503 --> 00:24:24.796
- Leaving the airlock.

00:24:26.882 --> 00:24:28.967
Hab, airlock is secure.

00:24:30.510 --> 00:24:32.596
- The primary
scientific objective

00:24:32.596 --> 00:24:35.056
of the first
research mission on Mars

00:24:35.056 --> 00:24:39.269
will be searching for signs
of life, past or present.

00:24:41.605 --> 00:24:44.858
People have been preoccupied
with finding life on Mars

00:24:44.858 --> 00:24:46.359
for centuries.

00:24:46.359 --> 00:24:48.195
In the 19th century,

00:24:48.195 --> 00:24:51.198
while mapping the red planet
with a telescope,

00:24:51.198 --> 00:24:54.201
Italian astronomer
Giovanni Schiaparelli

00:24:54.201 --> 00:24:56.912
saw what he thought
to be water channels

00:24:56.912 --> 00:24:58.330
and many believed they were

00:24:58.330 --> 00:25:02.501
artificially constructed
canals built by a Martian.

00:25:02.501 --> 00:25:05.253
But with the arrival
of the first Mariner missions

00:25:05.253 --> 00:25:08.924
to Mars in the '60s and '70s,
scientists confirmed

00:25:08.924 --> 00:25:12.594
that there was not
an advanced civilization.

00:25:12.594 --> 00:25:15.805
However, recent discoveries
of geological formations

00:25:15.805 --> 00:25:17.807
have excited them
about the possibility

00:25:17.807 --> 00:25:21.645
that some form of life
could have once existed.

00:25:22.938 --> 00:25:24.356
- All right,
so we're gonna get out now.

00:25:24.356 --> 00:25:27.359
- All right.

00:25:27.359 --> 00:25:29.903
- So we're heading for that
boulder on the second peak.

00:25:29.903 --> 00:25:33.490
- The geology surrounding
the MDRS is comparable

00:25:33.490 --> 00:25:36.451
to what the first researchers
on Mars will encounter.

00:25:36.451 --> 00:25:38.828
- Just wanna be on the lookout
for any minerals

00:25:38.828 --> 00:25:41.873
or interesting rocks
that we can evaluate.

00:25:41.873 --> 00:25:43.333
- To answer the question

00:25:43.333 --> 00:25:46.586
of whether primitive forms
of life existed on Mars,

00:25:46.586 --> 00:25:49.381
future researchers will look
to rocks.

00:25:49.381 --> 00:25:51.383
By peering inside these rocks,

00:25:51.383 --> 00:25:54.427
they may find the presence
of biomolecules,

00:25:54.427 --> 00:25:57.472
or even fossils of microbes.

00:25:57.472 --> 00:25:59.182
[exhales deeply]

00:25:59.182 --> 00:26:01.393
- We're at the top
of Kissing Camel Ridge.

00:26:01.393 --> 00:26:03.270
We're gonna mark the spot
on the GPS.

00:26:03.270 --> 00:26:04.938
Hab, I can give you
the grid reference now.

00:26:04.938 --> 00:26:10.026
4249540.
Over.

00:26:10.026 --> 00:26:12.904
- Yeah, this is the spot
for gypsum.

00:26:12.904 --> 00:26:17.075
- Gypsum is a mineral
that exists on Mars, as well.

00:26:17.075 --> 00:26:19.995
In 2011,
the Mars rover Opportunity

00:26:19.995 --> 00:26:23.164
discovered veins of gypsum
in the rock beds.

00:26:23.164 --> 00:26:26.751
Because gypsum forms in the
presence of water on Earth,

00:26:26.751 --> 00:26:29.546
Scientists believe that bodies
of water once flowed

00:26:29.546 --> 00:26:31.673
on the red planet.

00:26:31.673 --> 00:26:34.426
The team here in Utah
is simulating this search.

00:26:36.344 --> 00:26:38.054
- We're gonna
keep our eyes open.

00:26:38.054 --> 00:26:40.724
When it's covered in mud,
no one knows what it is.

00:26:40.724 --> 00:26:44.352
- On Earth,
gypsum has a practical use.

00:26:44.352 --> 00:26:47.314
It's used in the home
in walls and plaster.

00:26:47.314 --> 00:26:51.943
On Mars, it could also be used
for building new habitats.

00:26:51.943 --> 00:26:55.113
- So you'll get a lot
of gypsum on these clay beds.

00:26:55.113 --> 00:26:58.283
It's just pieces of rock
that push up.

00:26:58.283 --> 00:26:59.868
- That gypsum there?
- Yeah.

00:26:59.868 --> 00:27:01.578
Here's some gypsum, actually.

00:27:01.578 --> 00:27:03.913
We've got a little right here.

00:27:03.913 --> 00:27:05.373
Some is nice and clear
and pretty

00:27:05.373 --> 00:27:06.583
and some is really cloudy.

00:27:06.583 --> 00:27:08.126
- Hmm.

00:27:08.126 --> 00:27:10.462
- So we'll just mark
the location on GPS

00:27:10.462 --> 00:27:12.464
and then analyze them back
at the lab.

00:27:12.464 --> 00:27:15.800
- Sounds good.

00:27:15.800 --> 00:27:18.845
By examining the gypsum
in its surroundings on Mars,

00:27:18.845 --> 00:27:22.432
scientists might one day find
evidence of life

00:27:22.432 --> 00:27:26.853
and prove it also evolved
on the red planet.

00:27:26.853 --> 00:27:28.521
Back at the lab,

00:27:28.521 --> 00:27:31.858
the team processes
and examines the stone.

00:27:31.858 --> 00:27:32.859
- Part of the interesting thing

00:27:32.859 --> 00:27:33.985
about in being
in the simulation

00:27:33.985 --> 00:27:35.111
is you start thinking
through, like,

00:27:35.111 --> 00:27:36.529
well, what would we need
for this

00:27:36.529 --> 00:27:39.115
and what would we need
for that?

00:27:39.115 --> 00:27:42.285
But on Mars, everything has
to be either with you already

00:27:42.285 --> 00:27:44.996
or you have to, like,
cut up cans to make it work.

00:27:44.996 --> 00:27:46.164
- Right.

00:27:46.164 --> 00:27:48.416
But I can imagine
when we first visit Mars

00:27:48.416 --> 00:27:50.960
we're gonna have to have
scrappy operations like this.

00:27:50.960 --> 00:27:51.961
- Right.

00:27:51.961 --> 00:27:53.213
There's gonna be stuff that

00:27:53.213 --> 00:27:54.923
you're, like,
gonna have to make it work.

00:27:54.923 --> 00:27:57.258
And this is definitely
a great practice for that.

00:27:59.594 --> 00:28:01.262
- While some team members
are working out

00:28:01.262 --> 00:28:04.307
the technical challenges
of life off Earth,

00:28:04.307 --> 00:28:07.811
others are studying
the researchers themselves.

00:28:07.811 --> 00:28:12.190
PhD student Rich Whittle
is studying how human behave

00:28:12.190 --> 00:28:14.359
when confined
for long periods of time

00:28:14.359 --> 00:28:16.945
in aerospace environments
like these.

00:28:16.945 --> 00:28:18.655
- What we're looking at here
in particular

00:28:18.655 --> 00:28:20.323
is the psychology of crews

00:28:20.323 --> 00:28:22.367
in isolated, confined
environments.

00:28:22.367 --> 00:28:24.285
We're looking
at sleep quality,

00:28:24.285 --> 00:28:27.872
profile of moods,
positive and negative affect.

00:28:27.872 --> 00:28:31.751
- Will being cooped up in
isolation drive people crazy?

00:28:31.751 --> 00:28:33.128
Any mission to Mars

00:28:33.128 --> 00:28:35.922
would involve being away
from Earth for years.

00:28:35.922 --> 00:28:39.551
A one-way trip alone takes
about eight months

00:28:39.551 --> 00:28:41.845
and the first habitat
likely wouldn't be much larger

00:28:41.845 --> 00:28:44.556
than the space capsule's
tiny quarters.

00:28:44.556 --> 00:28:46.725
So I guess this
is one of the rooms, right?

00:28:48.518 --> 00:28:50.645
This is very humble.
[chuckles]

00:28:50.645 --> 00:28:54.149
- And then this
is probably the deluxe suite.

00:28:54.149 --> 00:28:56.484
- The commander's suite.
Oh, you can see light.

00:28:56.484 --> 00:28:59.154
This is luxury, man.
- I know.

00:28:59.154 --> 00:29:01.865
I'm feeling guilty.

00:29:01.865 --> 00:29:04.200
- Mark has experienced
the psychological impact

00:29:04.200 --> 00:29:07.620
of living
in isolation first hand.

00:29:07.620 --> 00:29:09.622
- I mean, Mark was stuck in
winter over in Antarctica.

00:29:09.622 --> 00:29:11.499
- Yeah?
- At South Pole, yeah.

00:29:11.499 --> 00:29:13.918
- So three months, or?
- Oh, no. A year.

00:29:13.918 --> 00:29:14.919
- Oh, wow.

00:29:14.919 --> 00:29:16.629
- It's a challenge.

00:29:16.629 --> 00:29:19.799
You're locked in with people.
You can't leave.

00:29:19.799 --> 00:29:23.553
That kind of confinement sort
of forces people apart.

00:29:23.553 --> 00:29:27.932
Towards the end, personality
conflicts started to come out.

00:29:27.932 --> 00:29:31.186
- One of the longest
Mars mission simulations

00:29:31.186 --> 00:29:33.062
took place in Russia.

00:29:33.062 --> 00:29:35.064
Called MARS-500,

00:29:35.064 --> 00:29:39.652
six volunteers were isolated
for 520 days.

00:29:39.652 --> 00:29:44.491
They even simulated the up
to 20-minute communication lag

00:29:44.491 --> 00:29:46.743
between Earth and Mars.

00:29:46.743 --> 00:29:49.913
The experiment showed
that prolonged confinement

00:29:49.913 --> 00:29:54.375
led to sleep disorders,
fatigue, and depression.

00:29:54.375 --> 00:29:57.086
Before the first crew
can be sent to Mars

00:29:57.086 --> 00:29:58.505
for the long haul,

00:29:58.505 --> 00:30:00.340
space agencies need
to figure out

00:30:00.340 --> 00:30:03.676
how to overcome the tedium,
personality conflicts,

00:30:03.676 --> 00:30:07.764
and emotional toll of extended
separation from loved ones.

00:30:07.764 --> 00:30:10.391
- Crews going to Mars
will be training together

00:30:10.391 --> 00:30:12.268
for at least a couple years,

00:30:12.268 --> 00:30:14.187
so they're going
to work out the kinks,

00:30:14.187 --> 00:30:16.147
they're going to find out
how to work together,

00:30:16.147 --> 00:30:18.149
'cause that's the real key

00:30:18.149 --> 00:30:20.443
in any kind
of situation like this.

00:30:20.443 --> 00:30:22.737
- With Mars simulations
like this,

00:30:22.737 --> 00:30:24.614
we'll definitely be able
to solve

00:30:24.614 --> 00:30:26.783
the technical and
psychological challenges

00:30:26.783 --> 00:30:29.202
of establishing a base on Mars

00:30:29.202 --> 00:30:34.165
and forge a path to the future
of life in space.

00:30:35.667 --> 00:30:37.043
narrator:
In the future,

00:30:37.043 --> 00:30:39.087
researchers
at the international Mars base

00:30:39.087 --> 00:30:41.840
make a remarkable discovery:

00:30:41.840 --> 00:30:45.009
A particular rock specimen
completely changes the way

00:30:45.009 --> 00:30:48.304
humanity thinks of its place
in the universe.

00:30:48.304 --> 00:30:51.307
The sample confirms
that ancient fossils

00:30:51.307 --> 00:30:55.603
similar to those found
on Earth also exist on Mars.

00:30:55.603 --> 00:30:57.397
This rock reveals that

00:30:57.397 --> 00:31:00.525
the complex forces
that cause life to evolve

00:31:00.525 --> 00:31:03.987
are not uncommon.

00:31:03.987 --> 00:31:05.989
In principle, life,

00:31:05.989 --> 00:31:09.158
possibly even complex
and intelligent life,

00:31:09.158 --> 00:31:13.371
may have evolved across
the universe.

00:31:16.207 --> 00:31:18.334
- While the first
research station on Mars

00:31:18.334 --> 00:31:21.045
is likely
to be small and scrappy,

00:31:21.045 --> 00:31:24.257
a long-term base with dozens
or even hundreds of people

00:31:24.257 --> 00:31:25.550
will need more in the way

00:31:25.550 --> 00:31:28.136
of complex facilities
and buildings.

00:31:28.136 --> 00:31:30.513
However,
transporting the materials

00:31:30.513 --> 00:31:33.433
to build large-scale,
heavy-duty habitats from Earth

00:31:33.433 --> 00:31:35.101
is unfeasible.

00:31:35.101 --> 00:31:37.186
How can we build
a permanent outpost

00:31:37.186 --> 00:31:40.023
using what's already
on the red planet?

00:31:40.023 --> 00:31:41.608
I'm in Brooklyn, New York,

00:31:41.608 --> 00:31:44.068
to meet a design team
with a unique solution.

00:31:44.068 --> 00:31:46.696
- So this is Mars Ice House.
- That's a habitat?

00:31:46.696 --> 00:31:48.197
- That's a habitat.
- Yeah.

00:31:48.197 --> 00:31:50.533
- Rebecca Pailes-Friedman
and Michael Morris

00:31:50.533 --> 00:31:52.493
run the forward-thinking firm

00:31:52.493 --> 00:31:54.871
Space Exploration
Architecture.

00:31:54.871 --> 00:31:58.374
Also called SEArch+,
they won NASA's international

00:31:58.374 --> 00:32:01.210
Mars habitat
design competition.

00:32:01.210 --> 00:32:04.923
- What we do is we design
habitats for humans

00:32:04.923 --> 00:32:07.008
to be in outer space.

00:32:07.008 --> 00:32:09.260
- We use indigenous materials,

00:32:09.260 --> 00:32:12.221
materials that are native
to the planet,

00:32:12.221 --> 00:32:15.391
and we create
a beautiful living experience

00:32:15.391 --> 00:32:18.519
that's also functional
for the astronauts.

00:32:18.519 --> 00:32:20.521
- But that's easier said
than done

00:32:20.521 --> 00:32:23.191
in the red planet's
lethal environment.

00:32:23.191 --> 00:32:27.612
- The primary goal will be
to protect against radiation

00:32:27.612 --> 00:32:32.283
from both solar rays as well
as cosmic galactic rays.

00:32:32.283 --> 00:32:35.370
The best material
to protect against radiation

00:32:35.370 --> 00:32:36.871
is actually water,

00:32:36.871 --> 00:32:38.915
which makes up most
of our atmosphere.

00:32:38.915 --> 00:32:43.544
- And Mars does have plenty
of water ice to work with.

00:32:43.544 --> 00:32:45.713
On Earth,
we're largely protected

00:32:45.713 --> 00:32:47.674
from harmful cosmic radiation

00:32:47.674 --> 00:32:50.176
thanks to our thick,
oxygen-rich atmosphere

00:32:50.176 --> 00:32:51.803
and magnetic fields.

00:32:51.803 --> 00:32:54.180
But with barely
any atmosphere,

00:32:54.180 --> 00:32:57.475
the surface radiation on Mars
is hazardous.

00:32:57.475 --> 00:33:02.355
High-energy particles from the
cosmic rays damage human DNA

00:33:02.355 --> 00:33:06.484
and can increase the risk
of diseases like cancer.

00:33:06.484 --> 00:33:09.404
- So like our atmosphere
protecting us here on Earth,

00:33:09.404 --> 00:33:12.407
we thought,
"Why not build an ice house?"

00:33:12.407 --> 00:33:14.701
We created
this double shell structure

00:33:14.701 --> 00:33:16.327
for additive protection

00:33:16.327 --> 00:33:18.788
but also
to have a bigger space.

00:33:18.788 --> 00:33:19.914
- That is so cool.

00:33:19.914 --> 00:33:22.125
- Part of our objective was,
like,

00:33:22.125 --> 00:33:24.085
if we go all the way to Mars,

00:33:24.085 --> 00:33:26.713
why should we live in a cave
or a concrete bunker?

00:33:26.713 --> 00:33:28.756
People should be able
to look outside,

00:33:28.756 --> 00:33:30.967
look at, you know, the
strangeness of the landscape,

00:33:30.967 --> 00:33:33.845
but they should also receive
the daylight

00:33:33.845 --> 00:33:35.972
which is very similar
to our own here on Earth.

00:33:35.972 --> 00:33:37.765
They have 25-hour days

00:33:37.765 --> 00:33:42.645
and they have about 50% of the
light we receive here on Earth.

00:33:42.645 --> 00:33:45.106
- But a long-term settlement
will need dwellings that

00:33:45.106 --> 00:33:48.943
are more complicated than what
can be built with just ice.

00:33:48.943 --> 00:33:50.903
The SEArch+ team has
a mind-blowing solution

00:33:50.903 --> 00:33:52.363
for that, too,

00:33:52.363 --> 00:33:57.243
using the mineral-rich
Martian soil, or regolith.

00:33:57.243 --> 00:33:59.203
- This is Mars X-House.

00:33:59.203 --> 00:34:02.832
X-House, the objective was
to build with Martian regolith,

00:34:02.832 --> 00:34:05.168
or concrete.

00:34:05.168 --> 00:34:08.755
We mined the exterior shell
from the soil

00:34:08.755 --> 00:34:11.174
and we mined
the interior pressure bubble

00:34:11.174 --> 00:34:12.717
out of high-density
polyethylene

00:34:12.717 --> 00:34:14.427
that we drew
from the atmosphere,

00:34:14.427 --> 00:34:16.429
so it's like
you're mining the sky

00:34:16.429 --> 00:34:18.681
and mining the soil
to create one building.

00:34:18.681 --> 00:34:21.601
- Another crucial innovation
is that this habitat

00:34:21.601 --> 00:34:25.396
is constructed before
the astronauts arrive.

00:34:25.396 --> 00:34:26.939
- It's built
by the time they get there

00:34:26.939 --> 00:34:28.232
and then when they get there,

00:34:28.232 --> 00:34:29.901
they can just do the work
that they need to do.

00:34:29.901 --> 00:34:33.196
- And the team had devised one
far-out method for building

00:34:33.196 --> 00:34:35.990
these prefabricated dwellings
remotely.

00:34:35.990 --> 00:34:37.283
- All the buildings
that we've been designing

00:34:37.283 --> 00:34:38.451
are all 3D-printed.

00:34:38.451 --> 00:34:40.953
- Wow, that is so cool.

00:34:40.953 --> 00:34:42.455
SEArch+'s plan calls

00:34:42.455 --> 00:34:45.917
for construction robots
to be sent to Mars in advance

00:34:45.917 --> 00:34:49.837
to build the habitats
using 3D printing technology.

00:34:49.837 --> 00:34:52.381
- There are no construction
workers on-site.

00:34:52.381 --> 00:34:54.300
We have to anticipate

00:34:54.300 --> 00:34:56.385
everything
that could possibly go wrong

00:34:56.385 --> 00:34:58.930
and design that beforehand.

00:34:58.930 --> 00:35:01.265
- But these
incredible structures

00:35:01.265 --> 00:35:03.518
aren't just computer models.

00:35:03.518 --> 00:35:05.019
The design team is working

00:35:05.019 --> 00:35:06.813
with state-of-the-art
3D printers

00:35:06.813 --> 00:35:09.982
to make versions of them
in real life.

00:35:09.982 --> 00:35:13.402
Using a mix of materials
similar to Martian soil,

00:35:13.402 --> 00:35:16.114
the printers build actual
free-standing versions

00:35:16.114 --> 00:35:18.032
of SEArch+'s structures.

00:35:18.032 --> 00:35:19.909
The future of this
breakthrough technology

00:35:19.909 --> 00:35:22.286
is much closer
than I could've imagined.

00:35:22.286 --> 00:35:23.871
- Like, we'll be
building a habitat

00:35:23.871 --> 00:35:26.374
on the surface of Mars by 2028.

00:35:26.374 --> 00:35:28.042
- Incredible.
And so--

00:35:28.042 --> 00:35:30.211
- So--I know that doesn't
sound, like, close,

00:35:30.211 --> 00:35:31.629
but that sounds pretty close
to us.

00:35:31.629 --> 00:35:33.422
- I mean--and that sounds
incredible to me, so.

00:35:33.422 --> 00:35:36.300
- [chuckles]
- That's absolutely amazing.

00:35:36.300 --> 00:35:39.303
Innovative designs
using materials found on Mars

00:35:39.303 --> 00:35:41.180
will pave the way
for the first settlers

00:35:41.180 --> 00:35:43.391
to arrive
to move-in ready homes.

00:35:45.184 --> 00:35:46.644
But what will happen

00:35:46.644 --> 00:35:48.980
when the supplies they brought
with them run out?

00:35:48.980 --> 00:35:51.440
They'll need food and oxygen
to survive

00:35:51.440 --> 00:35:54.443
in this alien,
hostile environment.

00:35:54.443 --> 00:35:57.113
For humans
to create civilizations

00:35:57.113 --> 00:35:58.698
anywhere in space,

00:35:58.698 --> 00:36:00.533
we'll need
to build self-contained

00:36:00.533 --> 00:36:02.618
and self-sustaining ecosystems

00:36:02.618 --> 00:36:04.787
that work like
our home planet.

00:36:04.787 --> 00:36:06.622
But how can
we build water cycles

00:36:06.622 --> 00:36:11.043
and farm in desert wastelands
like the moon or Mars?

00:36:11.043 --> 00:36:14.088
I'm outside Tucson, Arizona,
where researchers have been

00:36:14.088 --> 00:36:16.048
conducting decades
of ground-breaking research

00:36:16.048 --> 00:36:18.509
to achieve
this ambitious feat.

00:36:18.509 --> 00:36:21.345
- This is a grand experiment
in Earth sciences.

00:36:21.345 --> 00:36:23.347
Started about 30 years ago

00:36:23.347 --> 00:36:25.141
trying to understand
how the Earth works.

00:36:25.141 --> 00:36:27.476
They decided the only way
they could truly understand

00:36:27.476 --> 00:36:29.478
how the Earth controls itself

00:36:29.478 --> 00:36:32.523
is by creating
a facsimile of it.

00:36:32.523 --> 00:36:36.194
This is called Biosphere 2,
as we live in Biosphere 1.

00:36:39.989 --> 00:36:43.826
- Dr. Joaquin Ruiz
is the director Biosphere 2.

00:36:45.828 --> 00:36:47.330
It almost reminds me of, like,
a palace.

00:36:47.330 --> 00:36:48.664
[chuckles]

00:36:48.664 --> 00:36:50.333
Not, like, a laboratory
or research environment,

00:36:50.333 --> 00:36:53.294
but more like
a science palace.

00:36:53.294 --> 00:36:56.255
Can you tell us about what's
going on here in high level?

00:36:56.255 --> 00:36:57.381
- Sure.

00:36:57.381 --> 00:36:59.342
Right in front of us
is the rainforest.

00:36:59.342 --> 00:37:02.511
You have a big ocean
and then you have a desert.

00:37:02.511 --> 00:37:05.056
- It was an audacious idea.

00:37:05.056 --> 00:37:07.141
Build a small-scale version
of Earth

00:37:07.141 --> 00:37:10.061
in completely closed quarters.

00:37:10.061 --> 00:37:11.520
In a matter of years,

00:37:11.520 --> 00:37:14.523
they re-created
a wide array of ecosystems

00:37:14.523 --> 00:37:18.527
that originally took billions
of years on Earth to evolve.

00:37:18.527 --> 00:37:20.029
To succeed,

00:37:20.029 --> 00:37:24.242
Biosphere 2 needed to
be completely self-contained

00:37:24.242 --> 00:37:28.412
and sealed off with nothing
brought in or taken out.

00:37:28.412 --> 00:37:30.873
- We have to recycle
the nutrients,

00:37:30.873 --> 00:37:34.877
we're recycling our water,
our air.

00:37:34.877 --> 00:37:36.379
We're recycling everything.

00:37:36.379 --> 00:37:40.841
- In 1991, eight volunteers,
or Biospherians,

00:37:40.841 --> 00:37:45.096
lived inside Biosphere 2
for two full years.

00:37:45.096 --> 00:37:47.640
Like a future habitat on Mars,

00:37:47.640 --> 00:37:50.726
Biosphere 2 had
to be perfectly thought out

00:37:50.726 --> 00:37:52.478
and highly engineered.

00:37:52.478 --> 00:37:55.022
The health and safety
of future explorers

00:37:55.022 --> 00:37:57.233
depended on its success.

00:37:58.609 --> 00:38:00.152
Any habitat on Mars

00:38:00.152 --> 00:38:02.280
will need to remain
completely sealed off

00:38:02.280 --> 00:38:05.032
from the toxic environment
that surrounds it.

00:38:05.032 --> 00:38:07.868
But keeping it sealed
and structurally sound

00:38:07.868 --> 00:38:10.371
is a near-impossible task.

00:38:10.371 --> 00:38:12.581
Sudden changes
in internal air pressure

00:38:12.581 --> 00:38:15.167
could destroy
the structure itself.

00:38:15.167 --> 00:38:18.004
So the engineers
of Biosphere 2

00:38:18.004 --> 00:38:21.424
came up with this innovative
solution called the lung,

00:38:21.424 --> 00:38:25.469
and deputy director John Adams
takes me inside.

00:38:25.469 --> 00:38:28.431
- We're going into the lung
of Biosphere 2,

00:38:28.431 --> 00:38:30.433
and when
it was originally constructed,

00:38:30.433 --> 00:38:33.311
they designed it
to be hermetically sealed,

00:38:33.311 --> 00:38:35.855
but they knew they had to deal
with changes in pressure

00:38:35.855 --> 00:38:38.566
and this is the device
that they came up with

00:38:38.566 --> 00:38:40.776
to compensate for that.
- All right.

00:38:40.776 --> 00:38:42.778
[peaceful ambient music]

00:38:42.778 --> 00:38:45.406
Whoa.

00:38:45.406 --> 00:38:47.700
- This is just
a remarkable structure

00:38:47.700 --> 00:38:49.160
from
an engineering perspective.

00:38:49.160 --> 00:38:50.369
- [chuckles]

00:38:50.369 --> 00:38:52.580
I didn't expect to walk in
and see this.

00:38:52.580 --> 00:38:54.832
The lungs are connected
to the rest of the Biosphere

00:38:54.832 --> 00:38:56.334
by a series of tunnels

00:38:56.334 --> 00:38:59.378
to continuously balance
the air pressure inside.

00:38:59.378 --> 00:39:01.339
- What we're
standing inside of

00:39:01.339 --> 00:39:05.384
is an extremely large
diaphragm,

00:39:05.384 --> 00:39:06.844
and that diaphragm helps

00:39:06.844 --> 00:39:09.597
to modulate pressure
inside the facility.

00:39:09.597 --> 00:39:11.057
It has a counterweight,
an aluminum dish.

00:39:11.057 --> 00:39:12.058
- Yeah.

00:39:12.058 --> 00:39:13.309
- Flexible rubber membrane.

00:39:13.309 --> 00:39:16.771
The combined weight
of those two is 40,000 pounds.

00:39:16.771 --> 00:39:18.647
And the only thing
that's keeping it up in the air

00:39:18.647 --> 00:39:21.108
as you see it right now
is differential air pressure.

00:39:21.108 --> 00:39:23.903
- Wow.
That is pretty insane.

00:39:23.903 --> 00:39:25.905
[soft dramatic music]

00:39:25.905 --> 00:39:27.740
This weighted rubber diaphragm

00:39:27.740 --> 00:39:31.077
regulates the two basic kinds
of air pressure at work.

00:39:31.077 --> 00:39:33.329
As the temperature outside
heats up,

00:39:33.329 --> 00:39:37.124
the air inside the Biosphere
warms and expands.

00:39:37.124 --> 00:39:40.127
Inside the lungs,
the increasing air pressure

00:39:40.127 --> 00:39:43.547
pushes up against the
weighted, flexible diaphragm.

00:39:43.547 --> 00:39:45.674
Without the diaphragm's
flexibility,

00:39:45.674 --> 00:39:47.343
the air pressure
would shatter the glass

00:39:47.343 --> 00:39:49.178
of the sealed habitat.

00:39:49.178 --> 00:39:53.891
In space, an incident
like this would be deadly.

00:39:53.891 --> 00:39:57.395
As the air in the Biosphere 2
cools and contracts at night,

00:39:57.395 --> 00:40:00.398
the weight of the diaphragm
presses down.

00:40:00.398 --> 00:40:02.858
By reacting to the changing
of air pressure,

00:40:02.858 --> 00:40:06.153
the Biosphere 2's lungs
continuously adjust,

00:40:06.153 --> 00:40:07.947
applying the right amount
of force

00:40:07.947 --> 00:40:09.740
to keep the air pressure even

00:40:09.740 --> 00:40:11.909
throughout
the closed system habitat.

00:40:11.909 --> 00:40:16.080
- This is where air travels
back and forth

00:40:16.080 --> 00:40:20.668
as we modulate the pressure
changes within the facility.

00:40:20.668 --> 00:40:23.421
- On the red planet,
temperatures can fluctuate

00:40:23.421 --> 00:40:25.047
more than 100 degrees Celsius

00:40:25.047 --> 00:40:27.216
over the course
of a single day,

00:40:27.216 --> 00:40:30.386
which means a feature like
these lungs is essential

00:40:30.386 --> 00:40:33.222
to any future Mars habitats.

00:40:33.222 --> 00:40:34.640
And to sustain life,

00:40:34.640 --> 00:40:37.601
these habitats must cycle
and recycle the air

00:40:37.601 --> 00:40:41.355
for humans to breathe
and also grow plants for food.

00:40:41.355 --> 00:40:45.943
On Earth, this process links
animals and plants.

00:40:45.943 --> 00:40:49.613
Animals breathe in oxygen and
breathe out carbon dioxide.

00:40:49.613 --> 00:40:53.409
Plants absorb that CO2
and discharge oxygen.

00:40:53.409 --> 00:40:55.077
In the Biosphere,

00:40:55.077 --> 00:40:58.914
this fundamental cycle is
recreated with the rainforest.

00:40:58.914 --> 00:41:02.251
Researcher Laura Meredith
is conducting an experiment

00:41:02.251 --> 00:41:04.587
to test its resilience.

00:41:04.587 --> 00:41:07.131
- All right, so I'll show you
the rainforest.

00:41:07.131 --> 00:41:10.134
[peaceful music]

00:41:11.510 --> 00:41:15.014
So this is almost 30-year-old
tropical rainforest.

00:41:15.014 --> 00:41:17.016
- This is beautiful.
- Yeah, it's nice.

00:41:17.016 --> 00:41:19.685
Even three weeks into drought,
it's pretty green.

00:41:19.685 --> 00:41:21.353
Some of the trees
are dropping their leaves,

00:41:21.353 --> 00:41:23.564
the soils are drying out,
but really, you know,

00:41:23.564 --> 00:41:25.483
I think this rainforest
is showing us

00:41:25.483 --> 00:41:26.692
that it has quite a bit
of resilience.

00:41:26.692 --> 00:41:27.776
- Resilience.

00:41:27.776 --> 00:41:29.361
In the rainforest,

00:41:29.361 --> 00:41:32.198
plants absorb humans'
waste carbon dioxide

00:41:32.198 --> 00:41:33.824
the inhabitants breathe out.

00:41:33.824 --> 00:41:37.244
This subsequently helps the
plants grow and in the process

00:41:37.244 --> 00:41:40.915
also creates oxygen for
the inhabitants to survive.

00:41:40.915 --> 00:41:43.834
Growing a stable,
resilient plant ecosystem

00:41:43.834 --> 00:41:45.294
will be essential
to the survival

00:41:45.294 --> 00:41:49.131
of any future inhabitants
of the moon or Mars.

00:41:49.131 --> 00:41:52.676
However, the first
Biospherians weren't so lucky.

00:41:52.676 --> 00:41:56.847
16 months in, the oxygen
levels dropped precipitously.

00:41:56.847 --> 00:41:58.682
It turns out
there was something

00:41:58.682 --> 00:42:01.352
the original designers
underestimated:

00:42:01.352 --> 00:42:02.937
Microbes.

00:42:02.937 --> 00:42:06.232
Microbes in the soil
and in Biosphere 2's cement

00:42:06.232 --> 00:42:09.109
absorbed more oxygen
and released more CO2

00:42:09.109 --> 00:42:12.696
than originally estimated,
nearly leading to disaster.

00:42:14.156 --> 00:42:16.033
Fortunately
for the Biospherians,

00:42:16.033 --> 00:42:18.869
they intervened
and used additional machinery

00:42:18.869 --> 00:42:20.663
to pump in oxygen.

00:42:20.663 --> 00:42:24.792
In space, an oversight like
this would be catastrophic,

00:42:24.792 --> 00:42:26.210
which is why it's important

00:42:26.210 --> 00:42:28.754
to simulate and fully test
a system like this

00:42:28.754 --> 00:42:31.632
before building a biosphere
on another planet.

00:42:31.632 --> 00:42:33.384
- Because it's a closed system,

00:42:33.384 --> 00:42:36.136
you really need to understand
how your ecosystem works.

00:42:36.136 --> 00:42:38.931
Biology is really incredible
in its resilience

00:42:38.931 --> 00:42:40.683
and that's something that
you would really want to have

00:42:40.683 --> 00:42:43.352
as a component of your
ecosystem on another planet.

00:42:43.352 --> 00:42:46.605
- Can we create a closed living
system like this one on Mars?

00:42:46.605 --> 00:42:48.148
- It's an immense challenge

00:42:48.148 --> 00:42:51.151
to put together
all the complex components,

00:42:51.151 --> 00:42:54.697
but I am sure
that we can create one.

00:42:57.116 --> 00:42:59.410
- Thanks to the original
founders' dream,

00:42:59.410 --> 00:43:01.996
Biosphere 2 is a reality.

00:43:01.996 --> 00:43:03.414
And while this
living laboratory

00:43:03.414 --> 00:43:05.457
has had many challenges,

00:43:05.457 --> 00:43:07.793
Biosphere 2
is providing crucial insights

00:43:07.793 --> 00:43:11.922
into ways humanity can build
self-contained ecosystems.

00:43:11.922 --> 00:43:14.925
It's innovations like this
that will forge a path

00:43:14.925 --> 00:43:16.802
to future
long-term settlements

00:43:16.802 --> 00:43:19.263
on the moon or Mars.

00:43:19.263 --> 00:43:21.140
I think it's really gonna take

00:43:21.140 --> 00:43:24.101
the entirely
of society's focus

00:43:24.101 --> 00:43:26.770
for us to really start
expediting the process

00:43:26.770 --> 00:43:30.983
of getting people to Mars,
but I think one of the things

00:43:30.983 --> 00:43:34.862
that a few scientists
brought up that I spoke with

00:43:34.862 --> 00:43:37.823
was this idea
that we should focus

00:43:37.823 --> 00:43:41.410
on keeping our home here
habitable

00:43:41.410 --> 00:43:45.039
and not trying to run
to other places.

00:43:45.039 --> 00:43:47.958
Some argue,
"Why spend billions of dollars

00:43:47.958 --> 00:43:50.085
"to get to Mars when
we could be using this money

00:43:50.085 --> 00:43:53.464
"and ingenuity to solve
existing problems on Earth

00:43:53.464 --> 00:43:57.676
like poverty, disease,
and climate change?"

00:43:57.676 --> 00:43:59.511
- Should we flee this planet?

00:43:59.511 --> 00:44:01.639
People can't figure out
how to live wisely

00:44:01.639 --> 00:44:03.182
and live well on this planet.

00:44:03.182 --> 00:44:05.142
Think we've been given
quite a treasure here

00:44:05.142 --> 00:44:08.312
in this little
third rock from the sun.

00:44:08.312 --> 00:44:11.315
- But Kathryn also thinks
humans are destined

00:44:11.315 --> 00:44:13.525
to set foot
on the red planet someday.

00:44:13.525 --> 00:44:15.778
- I think it's certainly
technically possible

00:44:15.778 --> 00:44:18.030
to have a sustainable habitat
on Mars.

00:44:18.030 --> 00:44:20.824
And it's very--it's conceivable
that it could happen

00:44:20.824 --> 00:44:23.535
within the next
couple decades.

00:44:23.535 --> 00:44:25.496
- The technical challenges
are pretty immense.

00:44:25.496 --> 00:44:27.915
It's gonna take thousands
of people to work on this

00:44:27.915 --> 00:44:30.584
and concentrate on it
at the same time,

00:44:30.584 --> 00:44:32.378
but we feel that
it's important

00:44:32.378 --> 00:44:35.714
that humans see these planets
firsthand

00:44:35.714 --> 00:44:37.883
and we actually will
learn more.

00:44:37.883 --> 00:44:40.010
- Others look
at the greater good

00:44:40.010 --> 00:44:42.388
that can come
from new breakthroughs.

00:44:42.388 --> 00:44:44.682
- It's not gonna be cheap,
it's gonna be difficult,

00:44:44.682 --> 00:44:48.185
and we're gonna need
to push towards a common goal

00:44:48.185 --> 00:44:49.520
to get people to Mars.

00:44:49.520 --> 00:44:51.897
And hopefully beyond,
eventually.

00:44:51.897 --> 00:44:54.608
And every time we are able

00:44:54.608 --> 00:44:57.528
to accomplish something
like this,

00:44:57.528 --> 00:45:00.572
we can then bring
that technology back to Earth

00:45:00.572 --> 00:45:04.618
and the more advanced
these technologies get,

00:45:04.618 --> 00:45:07.162
the more advanced
that technologies on Earth get

00:45:07.162 --> 00:45:09.164
and I think
that's hugely beneficial.

00:45:09.164 --> 00:45:12.584
- And the discovery of life
elsewhere in the universe

00:45:12.584 --> 00:45:16.046
could have a profound impact
on humanity.

00:45:16.046 --> 00:45:19.258
- I think the odds of us being
the only life in the universe

00:45:19.258 --> 00:45:21.051
is very low.
[chuckles]

00:45:21.051 --> 00:45:22.845
I think there's life somewhere,

00:45:22.845 --> 00:45:24.263
so that's again why
it's critical for us

00:45:24.263 --> 00:45:25.806
to actually continue
to explore,

00:45:25.806 --> 00:45:27.266
to continue to look outward,

00:45:27.266 --> 00:45:28.976
because I do think eventually

00:45:28.976 --> 00:45:31.395
we're gonna find
somebody else.

00:45:31.395 --> 00:45:33.021
[upbeat music]

00:45:33.021 --> 00:45:36.400
- As a scientist that's long
dreamed of exploring space,

00:45:36.400 --> 00:45:39.069
I'm excited about overcoming
the challenges

00:45:39.069 --> 00:45:40.738
that will lead us to become

00:45:40.738 --> 00:45:44.408
a truly spacefaring
civilization.

00:45:44.408 --> 00:45:48.078
Getting humans on Mars and
settling and a civilization--

00:45:48.078 --> 00:45:50.706
I'd love to see that
in my lifetime.

00:45:50.706 --> 00:45:54.334
Will I see it in my lifetime?
I'm pretty sure.

